by nicolas-grekas at 2026-07-26T15:42:34Z

Reviewed and rebased onto the current 8.2. Thanks @eltharin, and sorry for the long wait.

I measured the effect end to end rather than reading the class hierarchy: real kernel, real `ControllerAttributesListener`, real firewall `ExceptionListener`, `debug=false`. With a `form_login` entry point the base branch answers 302 to /login and this PR answers 403; with no entry point, 401 becomes 403; with `http_basic`, the 401 and its `WWW-Authenticate` header become 403. Nothing produces a 500, and no path still returns 401. I also checked disclosure explicitly, since the exception is security-adjacent: the message is byte-identical, both renderers hide it when `debug=false`, and the JSON problem detail only moves from `"Unauthorized"` to `"Forbidden"`, which is status text and not the message.

@smnandre, you strongly disagreed on the grounds that CSRF is an authentication concern. The distinction that decides it for me is where the check runs. `CsrfProtectionListener` verifies the login form token during authentication and should keep throwing the `Security\Core` exception; `#[IsCsrfTokenValid]` guards a controller action reached by a caller who is already identified, and answering it with a login redirect is not useful to anyone. Both keep their own exception after this. Happy to keep talking if you still see it differently.

Three things fixed while amending:

* the CHANGELOG entry was in `SecurityBundle` and, after rebase, inside its 7.3 section. Nothing in SecurityBundle changes here, so it moved to `Security\Http` under 8.2. That was @kbond's request and it had not been applied.
* `UPGRADE-8.2.md` had no entry. Two things need one: old and new exception share no ancestor below `\RuntimeException`, so an application catching the `Security\Core` one for this case silently stops matching, and stateless firewalls go from 401 to 403.
* the test asserted only which exception class was thrown, so reparenting it onto something the firewall matches would have kept the suite green while restoring the bug. It now asserts the 403 status and that the exception is not an `AuthenticationException`, and it fails on a clean 8.2.

@nicolas-grekas, on your suggestion of `BadRequestHttpException`: I kept plain `HttpException`. `BadRequestHttpException` means 400, and forcing 403 through it requires bypassing its constructor, which reads oddly. `IsGrantedAttributeListener` throws a plain `HttpException` with a chosen status for the same kind of situation, so this matches the existing precedent in the component. Easy to change if you prefer otherwise.

@webda2l, the configurable `statusCode` you asked for is not in this PR. It is a reasonable follow-up now that the exception is an `HttpException`.

Local runs: Security\Http 637, Security\Core 399, SecurityBundle 378, all green. php-cs-fixer clean.

